## 1. Introduction

### 1.1 The problem of commensurability

What is the formal architecture of sustainability, viability, observation, governance, transformation, and composition — and what does that architecture prove about the assessment doctrines used to judge sustainability transitions?

Two failure modes motivate the question. The first is *commensurability drift*: sustainability assessments aggregate stocks, services, liabilities, and floors into single indices whose compensation principles are rarely stated as explicit mathematics. Genuine savings, inclusive wealth, and similar aggregate indicators treat natural, produced, human, and social capital as substitutable through prices, yet the conditions under which this substitution is valid — and the conditions under which it silently fails — are rarely formalized. The weak-sustainability doctrine holds that what matters is the aggregate value of the capital portfolio; the strong-sustainability doctrine holds that certain critical capitals must be maintained independently of aggregate wealth. This debate is mature in the policy literature (Neumayer, 2013; World Bank, 2011; Boos, 2015; Hanley et al., 1999; Ekins et al., 2003; Usubiaga-Liaño, 2025) and in multi-criteria decision analysis (Cinelli et al., 2014; Schär et al., 2025), but the *dynamic* consequences of the compensation assumption during a sustainability transition have received less formal attention.

### 1.2 The problem of status drift

The second failure mode is *status drift*: conceptual frameworks state aspirations as theorems, conditional results circulate as unconditional ones, and negative findings disappear from the literature. A framework that does not distinguish between what is stipulated, what is proved, what is conjectured, and what is counterexampled cannot audit its own claims. The framework introduced here was built against both failures. It assigns every object a type, every claim a status, and every cross-model statement a declared map — formalizing as distinct state spaces, proof obligations, and failure modes what assessment practice tends to treat as one currency.

### 1.3 Contribution and structure

This paper makes three contributions. First, it introduces a typed formal framework for sustainability assessment that stratifies uncertainty, fixes quantifier discipline, declares model maps, and classifies failure types. Second, it proves a separation theorem showing that the weak-sustainability assessment doctrine (scalarized aggregate) and the strong-sustainability assessment doctrine (noncompensatory typed) can disagree on the same transition system with the same robustness standard, in the direction weak-accepts/strong-rejects, on a set with interior — and that the disagreement is not an artifact of incorrect pricing but of quantifier noncommutativity. Third, it develops the architectural extensions — governance constructors, intergenerational structures, restricted composition interfaces, and negative-result methodology — that make the framework operational for policy analysis.

The paper proceeds as follows. Section 2 defines the typed canonical framework. Section 3 introduces the three assessment operators and the failure taxonomy. Section 4 states and proves the separation theorem. Section 5 interprets the result for the weak/strong sustainability debate. Sections 6–8 develop the governance, intergenerational, and composition extensions. Section 9 formalizes the negative-result methodology. Sections 10–12 address reproducibility, future directions, and limitations.

