### 5.4 Policy Implications of the Separation Theorem

The separation theorem has three direct implications for sustainability assessment and policy design.

**First: aggregate indices cannot certify transitions.** A sustainability assessment that reports only an aggregate index — genuine savings, inclusive wealth, or any scalarized portfolio measure — cannot distinguish between a transition that is safe under a single plan and a transition that is safe only because different price vectors license different plans. The distinction is invisible at the aggregate level. Theorem B proves that the region where the distinction matters has interior: it is not a knife-edge phenomenon but a structurally robust feature of the assessment geometry. Policy frameworks that rely exclusively on aggregate indicators for transition certification are therefore structurally unable to detect the separation. The implication is not that aggregate indicators are useless — Lemma 4.2 establishes that they are lossless at fixed trajectories — but that they are insufficient for certifying *transitions* between architectures.

**Second: fund the bridge, not the price.** The rescue/impossibility split of Theorem B(7) identifies the exact mechanism by which the gap can be closed. The impossibility region $I$ cannot be resolved by re-pricing the capitals: no price vector, and no family of price vectors, recovers the noncompensatory predecessor (Corollary A.1). The gap is closed only by funding the specific bridging resource — in the witness datum, the physical reserve $x$ at cost $c = 1$ — which converts the false positive into a certified transformation. The policy implication is direct: when a typed assessment identifies a false-positive region, the correct response is not to adjust the weights in the aggregate index but to identify and fund the specific resource that the typed recursion names as binding. The framework does not prescribe which resource to fund; it identifies *that* a resource is needed and *where* it binds.

**Third: require per-floor reporting alongside aggregate reporting.** The typed assessment requires each floor to be checked separately. Sustainability reports that provide only aggregate indices are structurally unable to detect the separation, because the separation exists precisely in the gap between the aggregate and the per-floor assessments. A minimal reporting standard implied by the theorem is: for every sustainability transition under assessment, report (i) the aggregate index at a declared set of weights, (ii) the per-floor status at each stage of the transition, and (iii) the typed assessment verdict. The first two are standard; the third is what the theorem shows is necessary. Without per-floor reporting, the assessment cannot distinguish between the rescue set and the impossibility region, and cannot identify the binding resource.

These implications are conditional on the declared datum: they apply to any transition system with the structure of the witness datum (multiple typed floors, asynchronous dips, price-dependent plan licensing). They do not apply to systems where a single plan is safe for all weights, in which case the assessments coincide and the separation is empty.

